  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">pybbi (2024) in Zenodo. 1 citation.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">pybbi (2024) in Zenodo is authored by Abdennur, Nezar. Topics: Computer science, Zoom, Artificial intelligence, Mathematics and Noise (video).</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">1 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include Nature Cell Biology.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "SoftwareStory",
  "publisher": "CERN",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">pybbi shows an early but credible uptake signal: it has 1 documented citation, including reuse in a 2025 article in <em>Nature Cell Biology</em>, and 1 recorded downstream reuse. The record suggests the software is already being taken up in genomics research, while broader attention metrics remain sparse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">pybbi is a Zenodo-archived software release by Nezar Abdennur, with versioned stewardship and an open-access distribution record that supports reuse. The impact evidence is still limited in volume, but it is notable in quality: the tool has already been cited in a 2025 research article in <em>Nature Cell Biology</em>, indicating adoption in a high-visibility life-sciences venue.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing paper sits in genomics and chromatin dynamics, with related concepts spanning nuclear structure and mitosis. That context is consistent with pybbi\u2019s role as infrastructure software rather than a standalone finding: its value appears to lie in supporting analysis workflows that are then carried into downstream biological research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Beyond formal citation, the record also shows 1 documented reuse in the software and article ecosystem. This is an early but meaningful signal for a software tool, because it suggests the release is being incorporated into subsequent scholarly work rather than only being archived.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the surrounding attention record is quiet. Datacite reports no views or downloads in the current digest, and there are no recorded online mentions or scite statements here. That pattern does not undermine the observed citation and reuse signals; it simply means the evidence base is still thin and concentrated in a small number of scholarly uses.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, pybbi\u2019s impact profile is best described as early-stage but promising: a versioned, openly deposited software tool with a clear trace of scholarly reuse, including one citation from a prominent journal in 2025. The current record points to practical utility within genomics research, with more evidence likely needed before any broader conclusions about reach can be drawn.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
}